h \af4 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe1033 \langfenp1033 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang2057 \langnp2057 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \cf1 Ovid\uc1 \u8217 ?s speeches are sometimes difficult to keep track of, because he will have a speaker telling us what someone else said, and that account may include more direct speech also containing direct speech. At one point, he has speeches within a speech within a speech within a speech. To avoid complex, awkward, and confusing punctuation, I have tried to stick to a simple use of quotation marks (double quotation marks for direct speech, and single quotation marks for all speeches within speeches) and have indented the left margin appropriately to indicate how direct or indirect a particular speech is.}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \cf1 \par }\pard \plain \s40 \ql \li0 \ri0 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx426 \tql \tx1276 \tql \tx1843 \tql \tx6521 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \par }\pard \plain \s40 \qc \li0 \ri0 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx426 \tql \tx1276 \tql \tx1843 \tql \tx6521 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 A SHORT INTRODUCTION}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \par }\pard \plain \s40 \ql \li0 \ri0 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx426 \tql \tx1276 \tql \tx1843 \tql \tx6521 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \par }\pard \plain \s37 \li0 \ri0 \sb0 \sa0 \sbauto0 \saauto0 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx426 \tql \tx1276 \tql \tx1843 \tql \tx6521 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 The }{\cs16 \i1 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af5 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 Metamorphoses}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105  by Publius Ovidius Naso has, over the centuries, been one of the most accessible and influential works from our classical past. This extraordinary collection of Greek and Roman myths and folk tales has always been a popular favourite and has decisively shaped Western art and literature from the moment it was completed in AD 8.}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \par }\pard \plain \s37 \li0 \ri0 \sb0 \sa0 \sbauto0 \saauto0 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx426 \tql \tx1276 \tql \tx1843 \tql \tx6521 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \par }\pard \plain \s37 \li0 \ri0 \sb0 \sa0 \sbauto0 \saauto0 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx426 \tql \tx1276 \tql \tx1843 \tql \tx6521 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 Ovid\uc1 \u8217 ?s stories, about 250 in all, begin with the creation of the world and end with a tribute to Augustus Caesar, the poet\uc1 \u8217 ?s contemporary, moving from famous early myths to fabulous divine or semi-divine heroic characters and }{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 well-known}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105  figures from traditional tales, then to the great historical saga of Troy, the wanderings of Aeneas and Ulysses}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105  (Odysseus)}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 , up to the recent history of Rome. Most of the stories involve miraculous and dramatically arresting transformations, in which a living being is changed into something else or is physically altered in some way, often as a result of intense suffering, or in which inanimate objects become living creatures.}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \par }\pard \plain \s37 \li0 \ri0 \sb0 \sa0 \sbauto0 \saauto0 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx426 \tql \tx1276 \tql \tx1843 \tql \tx6521 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \par }\pard \plain \s37 \li0 \ri0 \sb0 \sa0 \sbauto0 \saauto0 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx426 \tql \tx1276 \tql \tx1843 \tql \tx6521 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 The work has always been one of our principal sources of classical stories and hence an invaluable resource for later poets, painters, playwrights and storytellers, from countless medieval and }{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 Renaissance}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105  artists (including Shakespeare) to modern Broadway producers. But the popularity and influence of the poem rest on a great deal more than the outlines of the tales. For Ovid has a brilliant style, which transforms what might otherwise be a mere catalogue of stories into a seamless, forward-driving, and endlessly diverting totality.}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \par }\pard \plain \s37 \li0 \ri0 \sb0 \sa0 \sbauto0 \saauto0 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx426 \tql \tx1276 \tql \tx1843 \tql \tx6521 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \par }\pard \plain \s37 \li0 \ri0 \sb0 \sa0 \sbauto0 \saauto0 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx426 \tql \tx1276 \tql \tx1843 \tql \tx6521 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 Ovid is famous for his skill in creating transitions from one story to the next, often by having a character in one story move onto something new in the next, or by following someone\uc1 \u8217 ?s reaction to a story. He will frequently arrange stories in groups as part of an ongoing conversation or contest, or a series of stories within a story. As a result, the poem never flags or develops a stop-start rhythm but is always urging the reader or listener forward in one continuous narrative.}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \par }\pard \plain \s37 \li0 \ri0 \sb0 \sa0 \sbauto0 \saauto0 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx426 \tql \tx1276 \tql \tx1843 \tql \tx6521 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \par }\pard \plain \s37 \li0 \ri0 \sb0 \sa0 \sbauto0 \saauto0 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx426 \tql \tx1276 \tql \tx1843 \tql \tx6521 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 Ovid also likes to anchor his stories in the passionate emotions of the gods or of human individuals, so that the actions arise out of familiar and powerful feelings. The poem is justly famous for its set speeches, especially those of a young girl in love for the first time and caught up in passions which she knows she should resist but cannot, invariably with disastrous results. The psychological complexities many of the characters reveal in their self-examinations are fascinating. The formal speeches, too, like the long speech of Pythagoras or Medea\uc1 \u8217 ?s famous incantation or Hecuba\uc1 \u8217 ?s lament, bring these characters to life and have helped to define them for hundreds of years. This intensely dramatic quality allows us to feel the stories much more immediately, so that we can make up our own minds, rather than having to listen to the judgment of the narrator. The speeches also bring out evocatively the harsh cruelty of many of the transformations, which are usually the final stage of intense suffering.}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \par }\pard \plain \s37 \li0 \ri0 \sb0 \sa0 \sbauto0 \saauto0 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx426 \tql \tx1276 \tql \tx1843 \tql \tx6521 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \par }\pard \plain \s37 \li0 \ri0 \sb0 \sa0 \sbauto0 \saauto0 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx426 \tql \tx1276 \tql \tx1843 \tql \tx6521 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 Ovid is famous, too, for his visual imagination, which ranges from cosily erotic and often humorous details, to graphic depictions of natural disasters, like storms at sea, catastrophic floods or plagues, to an almost grotesque treatment of battle wounds and mutilations. Given the imaginative quality of these images, it is no accident that the poem has had such a major influence on the history of Western art.}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \par }\pard \plain \s37 \li0 \ri0 \sb0 \sa0 \sbauto0 \saauto0 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx426 \tql \tx1276 \tql \tx1843 \tql \tx6521 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \par }\pard \plain \s37 \li0 \ri0 \sb0 \sa0 \sbauto0 \saauto0 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx426 \tql \tx1276 \tql \tx1843 \tql \tx6521 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 But the most famous, complex, and contested aspect of Ovid\uc1 \u8217 ?s poem is the narrator\uc1 \u8217 ?s attitude to his own stories. The speaker (who clearly identifies himself as the poet) repeatedly calls attention to the absurdity of the fictions he is creating and mocks the credulity of people who believe them. In many places Ovid pushes the descriptive details to such an extreme that one gets a sense he may be ridiculing his own stories and also earlier works which have dealt with similar events, simultaneously paying a tribute to a famous predecessor and gently }{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 satirizing}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105  him. It is as if he is inviting us to enjoy the poem but not to take it all that seriously.}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \par }\pard \plain \s37 \li0 \ri0 \sb0 \sa0 \sbauto0 \saauto0 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx426 \tql \tx1276 \tql \tx1843 \tql \tx6521 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \par }\pard \plain \s37 \li0 \ri0 \sb0 \sa0 \sbauto0 \saauto0 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx426 \tql \tx1276 \tql \tx1843 \tql \tx6521 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 Now, this is a risky business. After all, if the narrator is constantly threatening to pull the carpet out from under his own fiction, there is a real danger that the reader might simply lose interest or get confused. But Ovid negotiates this difficulty with consummate skill: he delights us with his stories and, at the same time, constantly mocks the notion, directly and indirectly, that they offer a significant insight into anything. The technique has the effect of driving something of a wedge between the style and the content and invites us to admire the amazing skill with which the poet can create such a remarkable work, so much so that, in a very real sense, the poet\uc1 \u8217 ?s skill, his extraordinary verbal \uc1 \u8216 ?wit\uc1 \u8217 ?, is one of the central attractions of the poem, constantly on display.}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \par }\pard \plain \s37 \li0 \ri0 \sb0 \sa0 \sbauto0 \saauto0 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx426 \tql \tx1276 \tql \tx1843 \tql \tx6521 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \par }\pard \plain \s37 \li0 \ri0 \sb0 \sa0 \sbauto0 \saauto0 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx426 \tql \tx1276 \tql \tx1843 \tql \tx6521 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 Unlike the works of the great epic poets he is drawing on, Ovid\uc1 \u8217 ?s poem does not invite us to treat the stories, individually or collectively, as presenting anything we might consider a vision of life. They matter as stories, for they provide him with an opportunity to display his poetical skill, but they do not put pressure on us to think about anything beyond this literary concern. That, indeed, may be one key to his popularity, for Ovid\uc1 \u8217 ?s attitude to his myths neutralizes any threat they might pose to a rival system of belief. Some of those who demand that a poet should strive for a high moral seriousness have deplored this aspect of Ovid\uc1 \u8217 ?s style, citing his inappropriately sly tone or the pleasure he takes in rhetorical excess. Others, more sensitive to the delights of poetry, have always considered Ovid a poet\uc1 \u8217 ?s poet, someone we read in order to surrender ourselves to the sheer pleasures of delightful fiction for its own sake. That, as Ovid seems to have sensed, is the key to his enduring popularity: in a world of constant change, his closing lines proclaim, emperors may come and go, but his brilliant work will always remain.}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \par }\pard \plain \s37 \li0 \ri0 \sb0 \sa0 \sbauto0 \saauto0 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx426 \tql \tx1276 \tql \tx1843 \tql \tx6521 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 Finally, a short note on the selections (which are presented in the order in which they occur in Ovid\uc1 \u8217 ?s complete poem). I have concentrated on including passages that, first, tell a good story more or less on their own, without a potentially irritating thicket of explanatory footnotes (of the sort that would be required for the debate between Ajax and Ulysses or the wanderings of Aeneas, for instance), and, second, that are }{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \f0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 excellent examples of Ovid\uc1 \u8217 ?s poetic style at its finest. I also kept in mind the usefulness of a story for the reader\uc1 \u8217 ?s understanding of important later literary references. }{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \f0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \par }\pard \plain \s37 \li0 \ri0 \sb0 \sa0 \sbauto0 \saauto0 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx426 \tql \tx1276 \tql \tx1843 \tql \tx6521 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \f0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \par }\pard \plain \s37 \li0 \ri0 \sb0 \sa0 \sbauto0 \saauto0 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx426 \tql \tx1276 \tql \tx1843 \tql \tx6521 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \f0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 Given these priorities, many selections virtually picked themselves (e.g., the Transformation of Chaos, Apollo and Daphne, Pygmalion, Pha\uc1 \u235 ?ton, Icarus, Pyramus and Thisbe, and so on). I also considered it important to offer at least one longer section incorporating several stories (the Songs of Orpheus) in order to offer }{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \f0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 a}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \f0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105  lengthy example of Ovid\uc1 \u8217 ?s skill at creating stories in combination and transitioning seamlessly from one to another). I chose at least one tale that is not at all well known (Cinyras and Myrrha) because it is part of that sequence and because it is such a splendid example of Ovid\uc1 \u8217 ?s style, especially the attitude of }{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \f0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 the narrator}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \f0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105  to his own fiction and the treatment of a young girl wrestling with powerful sexual feelings).}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \f0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \par }\pard \plain \s37 \li0 \ri0 \sb0 \sa0 \sbauto0 \saauto0 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx426 \tql \tx1276 \tql \tx1843 \tql \tx6521 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \f0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \par }\pard \plain \s37 \li0 \ri0 \sb0 \sa0 \sbauto0 \saauto0 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx426 \tql \tx1276 \tql \tx1843 \tql \tx6521 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \f0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 There are, of course (and regrettably), many }{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \f0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 well-known}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \f0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105  stories I have not included. In some cases I have done so because the passage is too long or too repetitive (e.g., the battle of the Lapiths and Centaurs) or because the style lacks much of the \uc1 \u8220 ?wit\uc1 \u8221 ? that }{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 Ovid is most celebrated for (e.g., the accounts of the achievements of Aeneas or Romulus or Julius Caesar) or because the passage requires extensive footnoting to provide necessary background information (e.g., the debate between Ajax and Ulysses).  Others I would have happily included if I had not faced limitations on the length of the book. I offer my sincere apologies to any readers whose particular favourite has not been included.}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \par }\pard \plain \s37 \li0 \ri0 \sb0 \sa0 \sbauto0 \saauto0 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx426 \tql \tx1276 \tql \tx1843 \tql \tx6521 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 \par }\pard \plain \s40 \ql \li0 \ri0 \widctlpar \nooverflow \aspalpha \aspnum \faauto \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \tql \tx426 \tql \tx1276 \tql \tx1843 \tql \tx6521 \tql \tx8505 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af0 \loch \af0 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 The selections are all taken from my translation of the complete }{\i1 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 Metamorphoses }{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 (}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105 combined,}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af0 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe4105 \langfenp4105  they represent about one third of the total poem). In many cases, the selections have been edited to make them somewhat shorter than in the original. Should thes